            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Causes of the Six-Day War</strong><br>\nThe Six-Day War, which took place in 1967, had many causes. One of the main reasons was the political tension between Israel and its Arab neighbors. These tensions were exacerbated by territorial disputes and conflict over access to vital water resources. In addition, there was the constant threat of terrorist attacks and mutual animosity between Israel and the Arab states.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Start of the War</strong><br>\nThe war began on June 5, 1967, when Israel carried out preemptive air strikes against Egypt, Jordan, and Syria. These attacks were highly effective and destroyed a significant portion of the Arab states' air forces. As a result, Israel was able to strengthen its position and was better prepared for the subsequent battles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Course of the War</strong><br>\nIn the following days, Israel attacked the ground forces of the Arab states and captured significant territories. These included the Sinai Peninsula, the Golan Heights, the West Bank, and the eastern part of Jerusalem. The Israeli forces were well-trained and better equipped, giving them a decisive advantage.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>International Reactions</strong><br>\nThe international community had mixed reactions to the war. Some countries supported Israel, while others supported the Arab states. The United Nations called for a ceasefire, which came into effect on June 10, 1967, ending the war.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Israel</strong><br>\nThe impact of the Six-Day War on Israel was enormous. Through the conquest of new territories, Israel significantly expanded its territory. This strengthened Israel's strategic position but also brought new security challenges, including the occupation and administration of the captured territories and relations with Palestinian residents.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Arab States</strong><br>\nFor the Arab states, the war was a sobering experience. They suffered considerable military and territorial losses. This led to political changes in some Arab countries and intensified hostility toward Israel.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
